he immune cells  so  in children it's very difficult to do  bronchoscopies um it's and get airway  epithelial cells and for this reason a  number of researchers have started using  nasal epithelial cells as a surrogate  for um  airway epithelial cells  so in a seminal paper  published by max cybold's group at  national jewish one of my colleagues it  was shown that uh the nasal  transcriptome um is very similar to the  airway transcriptome and that this um  also that there are changes associated  with childhood asthma in the nasal  transcriptome that are similar  to uh what happens in the arab  transcriptome  um  at the same time there was another study  um by andrea carelli's group that  showed uh differences in dna methylation  in nasal cells of children with asthma  compared to controls  and similarly a  paper from pedro avila's group  showing that human rhinovirus infection  causes different meth causes different  dna methylation changes in nasal  epithelia of asthmatics compared to  controls  so all of these uh publications um  demonstrated that the nasal epithelium  is a good surrogate for the airway  epithelium um and we started looking at  um the epithelial nasal epithelial cells  in our cohorts  um so the way we did this is um we  were able to collect uh nasal epithelial  samples on about half of the subjects  that were involved enrolled in our  original population of 97 cases and 97  controls that i  talked about in the pbmc study  of those  um we selected 36 cases and 36 controls  that have most pure columnar epithelia  and this was accessed by fox j1 gene  expression and fox j1 is a transcription  factor that's very specific to uh  ciliated cells  so the reason we do this is that we're  really interested when you do a nasal  brushing sometimes you get a mix of  squamous and uh columnar epithelia and  we're really only interested in columnar  epithelia because the those are the  cells that uh represent that are similar  to the area epithelia so we use this  to identify only samples that are really  representative of the airway epithelia  when we  perform the genome-wide methylation  profiling  we did this in the same way that we did  it  in the pbmc study as well as in the ige  uh study so it's illumina 450k  methylation arrays  and this analysis identified a stronger  signal than what we found in the  pbmcs although our population was  smaller  so we identified 119  differentially methylated regions or  dmrs that's plotted  in the panel a of the figure on the  manhattan and we also identified 118  single cpg sites that are associated  after adjustment for multiple  comparisons  and what i should tell you that i didn't  tell you before in all our analyses  um we run uh linear models and we adjust  for confounders such as age gender and  ethnicity and race because we know that  these um  can influence methylation in addition to  that we do a lot of quality control and  we  deal with batch effects before we get to  statistical models  so what are some of the genes that are  differentially methylated in the nasal  epithelia  um  if you look at the differentially  methylated regions and differentially  methylated probes or positions so these  are again regions of sustained low p  values  associated with  asthma  or  single cpg sites that are significant  after adjustment for multiple  comparisons  um there's some overlap  which is not surprising um  there are about 40 loci that overlap but  there are 70 to 80 that are unique to  the two analyses and i think this really  illustrates that it's important to look  at the data in multiple ways  when you do these genomic profiles  when we take the union of this venn  diagram that i'm showing you we identify  a number of genes that we know are  important in asthma  so um alox 15 is one of the genes that's  been associated with asthma extensively  kelpines um histamine and metal  transferase is important in the allergic  part so allergy periosteum is  differentially expressed in the airway  epithelium of  asthmatics and it's a biomarker that's  that is being used for asthma  we also see changes in methylation of  genes related to extracellular matrix  immunity cell adhesion airway  obstruction  obesity which is also associated with  asthma so there is a subtype of asthma  that's related to obesity  autophagy so again these are all  cellular processes that we know are  important  in asthma  um when we compare these loci to those  that we identified in pvmcs only four of  them overlapped and they're not very  exciting genes that we know are  important in asthma so what this really  tells you is how important it is to look  at different samples um and this is not  surprising given that we're looking at  epithelial cells versus um more immune  cells in the pbmcs  um so it's not surprising that  methylation changes are different given  what we know about cell specificity of  epigenetic marks  these are just some representative plots  of  differentially methylated regions  that we identified as  associated with asthma so on the y-axis  are is the methylation change and then  um  on the x-axis is genomic coordinate so  for example methyltransferase like juan  med1 has only one probe that's um  associated with asthma  versus um  mhc2 dp alpha one for example has a  number of probes so that's what's on the  x-axis uh what this histogram is of um  is of the methylation marks in the  percent methylation estimate in 36 cases  and 36 controls  so um controls are in red cases are in  blue and what you can see is that uh  three of the four representative loci  that i'm showing you're hypomethylated  and asthma so less methylation in the  blue compared to red  versus um the hla dpa1 locus is  hypermethylated so more methylation  i'm hoping that you can see the y-axis  um and um i'll also be able to  illustrate this point on the next slide  but um  what's important here is that these  methylation changes are larger so when  we looked at in pbmcs we found  methylation changes between asthmatics  and controls under order of one to five  percent so very small changes very  consistent but small when we look in  nasal epithelia these are changes from  ranging from 10 to 30 percent and if you  look at uh epigenomic profiles in cancer  these methylation change differences are  approaching those identified in cancer  so  again this is um these are big changes  um because these are big changes it was  very easy to validate both internally so  technical validation and externally by  pyrosequencing  um similar to our pbmc study we also  looked at a relationship of dna  methylation and expression um and this  this is exact same layout of the plots  well i just have two plots because i  have regions and a single  um  probes single cpg sites and you can see  that there is um an enrichment so on  this graph i actually do have the p  values for the enrichment  of um  hypomethylated or  of inverse correlations of  methylation and gene expression  as we would expect  we also analyze the data slightly  differently focusing on what is  differentially expressed so we  use genomic arrays to look at gene  expression and identified for 53 genes  that are significantly differentially  expressed in asthmatics compared to  controls  many of the genes that are  differentially expressed are  those that we know are important in  biology of asthma i mentioned alex 15 is  differentially methylated it's also  differentially expressed  there are a number of other genes that  are related to asthma and allergy as  well as immunity  and some that have been shown to be  consistently differentially expressed  across multiple studies  and some that are used as biomarkers  so looking at these 53 genes and this is  just the plot that i'm showing you is uh  plots the  expression uh for asthmatics on the  y-axis and on the controls on the x-axis  um  the  red dots are genes that are not  differentially expressed and then blue  dots are the ones that are significantly  differentially expressed with the  the size of the dot being proportional  to the full change so difference in gene  expression  so what we wanted to do while we're  interested in expression we're really  mostly interested is in how dna  methylation regulates this expression  response  um so  what we did is we um  looked at um methylation a relationship  of ventilation and expression  quantitatively  just in these 53 genes  and after adjusting for multiple  comparisons we found that 32 out of 53  so 60 of the genes have significant  methylation expression relationships  this is a significant enrichment uh  compared to if we compare  genome-wide how many significant  methylation expression relationships we  have there's an enrichment in these  genes that are differentially expressed  and these are predominantly canonical  inverse relationships 25 out of 32.  um to begin to understand uh the biology  of  these 32 genes uh we performed the  network analysis using protein-protein  interaction data  from the  um  from the innate database of interactomes  and using the network analyst  tool that's available online  in this  network graph  genes that are  upregulated in expression and  hypomethylated so less methylation more  expression  are in red and inversely genes that are  downregulated in expression and  hypermethylated in terms of methylation  are in green  and what you can see in this graph is  that we identified a number of modules  within this network  with genes that we know are important in  asthma  so this um rpk2 gene or ripk2 gene is  really important in innate immune  response in relation to um asthma and  other immune diseases  ccl5 is a chemokine um and so on and  some of the hla genes are in here as  well  um so again this really illustrates how  network analysis can help you find  modules of important uh genes with both  changes in differential expression and  methylation  so what are some of the conclusions that  we can draw from the studies that i've  shown you so far  uh well i think i've shown you that  methylation marks regulate serum ig  concentrations and peripheral blood um  of asthmatic and non-asmat compared to  non-asthmatic children  um i showed you that methylation of  pbmcs  and nasal epithelia are associated with  allergic asthma in inner city children  um  i did  it's important to remember that  methylation marks in pvmcs and nasal  epithelia are different uh which is  again not surprising given how different  these cell types are  um  importantly i showed you that asthma  associated changes in methylation of  nasal epithelia have a much larger  signal in comparison to pbmcs so when uh  looking at um  we know that we can't look at the lung  of children with  asthma but in identifying surrogate  tissue the  tissue or sample that we can use  nasal cells are preferable to pvmcs  um i've shown you in all these studies  that methylation changes are associated  with changes in gene expression  and in my last study of the nasal  epithelia the genes that are expressed  by the nasal epithelium and asthmatic  children appear to be epigenetically  regulated  so while we think epigenetics is really  important it is also important to  remember that epigenetics is just one of  the or the epigenome is just one of the  pieces of the puzzle  and this slide i think demonstrate that  that really well  um that it's important to look at  the epigenome the genome transcriptome  but it's also  microbiome  nutrition and other  pieces that  are important in asthma  so to start to be able to focus on the  environment  and a microbiome uh we  just started a study  um in monozygotic twins and why use  twins well the reason that we are using  twins is that this really allows us and  especially monozygotic twins is this  allows us to remove the effect of  genetics and it allows us to focus more  on the environment  small epigenomic studies in twins in  other diseases and one um that's been  published in asthma has shown increased  power to detect associations  and then using both and monozygotic and  dizygotic twins would also allow us to  estimate heritability of  asthma-associated ventilation marks  um so as i mentioned this is a study  that we're really just beginning and i  have just the one slide a very  preliminary data but i think they're  exciting data and so i wanted to share  them  so  in a small study of all monozygotic twin  pairs  10  discordant for asthma 10 concordant for  asthma so both have asthma and 10  concordant for non-asthma so both twins  are not asthmatic  um we performed a genome-wide  methylation study again using 450  methylation erased from illumina the  same platform that i've talked about  throughout the talk  and in this study we identified 62  differentially methylated regions in  nasal epithelia that are associated with  asthma  what we thought was really interesting  is that there is  um enrichment um so high percentage of  genes um in the 62 loci  are  associated with the nean immunity we did  not see this in the other study and we  think it's because we are able now to  focus in on this innate immune response  to the environment  because we've removed the effect of  genetic genetics  um  in very preliminary analyses half of the  dmrs so differentially methylated  regions are associated with difference  in endotoxin levels into bedding dust  so endotoxin levels in the bedding dust  are um an environmental exposure and the  toxin is part of grain negative bacteria  and so it shows uh  what is um going on in the environment  and we also in collaboration with kathy  lozapone a fantastic  microbiome researcher here at the  university of colorado we looked at uh  the microbiome in the nasal epithelial  cells so in the same sample of these  children and we also were able to show  that half of the dmrs are also  associated with difference in abundance  of some hydrocarbon degrading bacteria  in the nose  so again um this is very preliminary  very early but i think it really  illustrates  how  the right study design in term in this  case using monozygotic twin pairs can  help you identify  important  epigenetic marks um when you're trying  to focus on the environment  so what are the implications of um what  i've told you about today  um so we think methylation marks  especially in the nasal epithelia could  be used as biomarkers of asthma  however  it is really important to realize that  these studies are very early and that we  need to better understand  the relationship of these methylation  marks to disease activity  allergy exposures and treatments  um  they also we think are novel targets for  understanding biology of asthma  especially given their relationship to  gene expression  and their potential therapeutic targets  since methylation can be modified  so let me finish up by just telling you  a little bit about epigenetic therapies  um this is a summary slide from our  recent or relatively recent review  article that shows you all the um  therapeutics that are available for  affecting dna methylation and histone  modifications  um so if you focus on dna  methyltransferase inhibitors there are a  number of them they have been in  clinical trials and  cancers  they have not been  looked at in the context of  non-malignant diseases yet but they are  available  one of the problems with these dna  methyltransferase inhibitors is that  they are non-specific so they will  dimethylate the entire genome or many  loci in the genome not just the locus  that you're interested in demethylating  some of the history modifying  therapies are  more  specific in the sense that they target  the pathway  however none of them are specific to the  point that we would want to say we would  like to target for example just th two  genes and asthma  however there's a lot going on in terms  of using uh genome editing technologies  to develop locus-specific epigenetic  therapies and i'm just listing three  studies um that you can look more into  in terms of how other labs are starting  to  use genome editing  techniques such as crispr cas9  technology to um so what they do is they  fuse the  crispr domain to a histone or dna  modifying enzyme domain and are able to  affect methylation or histone  modification just at a specific locus  and it's really important to know that  this is very early on in terms of  development of these locus-specific  therapies but  i think that they will be available in  the future and um we should um  by then we should know a lot more  answers about how important epigenetic  marks are in asthma um and hopefully  have all the results that would  enable us to use some of these therapies  in the future  so with that i would like to finish my  presentation i would like to acknowledge  individuals who have contributed to this  work and there are a lot of them  um first of all in our laboratory bet  davidson and karen hennessy did all the  lab work brent patterson is a very  talented bioinformatician who did all  the analyses  um  in terms of the inner city asthma  consortium studies so these are children  from the inner city with asthma um  there are a number of collaborators  these samples were collected across the  country um and these are just the  collaborators who have participated um  the mrca cohort so the study that i  mentioned um in terms of a relationship  of ige crm ige and dna methylation  was done uh by bill cooks and miriam  alpha and les mingliand uh we were  collaborators in this study  and then finally um the early studied uh  the early results that i showed you from  our twin cap study so this is study of  twins in colorado and in relation to  asthma i would like to acknowledge those  investigators as well  thank you for your attention and i will  be happy to answer questions  thank you for the informative  presentation before we get started on  the question and answer session i would  like to remind our audience how to  submit questions you can submit  questions by typing them in the q a box  which can be found by clicking on the  green q a button at the lower left of  the presentation window  our first question reads  not being in the area of genetics my  question is why do we get dysregulation  and methylation in the first place are  there publications addressing this  question in any disease condition  that's a really good question um there  are a lot of publications um  the as i mentioned early on really the  longest the disease that this has been  studied the longest in is cancer  but other diseases um so if you just go  to pubmed and put  dna methylation and diabetes dna  methylation and  obesity dna methylation and i don't know  pick a disease rheumatoid arthritis you  will see that there are now publications  showing association of dna methylation  and  changes in dna methylation and  disease development  why do they happen they're dysregulated  probably for a variety of reasons  some of them are in utero exposures so  um  i use the example of cigarette smoke but  in another study that i'm doing we're  finding this regulation of dna  methylation  in kids whose mothers had gestational  diabetes during pregnancy  they can be  dysregulated by  environmental exposure so if you live in  a polluted city  there may be dysregulation  they are really focusing on the  environmental piece  but also there are publications that  demonstrate that genetic factors can  also regulate dna methylation  so if you have a genetic variant  in a specific place in a genome  methylation marks around that genetic  variant might be altered by the present  of that  variant  great so it looks like that was the only  submitted question so we'll go ahead and  wrap it up today's webcast will be  available for on-demand viewing through  november 2015. you will receive an email  from labyrinths alerting you when this  webcast will be available for replay  we we invite you to forward that  announcement to your colleagues who may  have 